WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.631
Homomorphic encryption lets a server

2
00:00:02.696 --> 00:00:04.380
compute on encrypted data.

3
00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:06.754
CKKS computes on encrypted

4
00:00:06.832 --> 00:00:08.311
vectors of numbers

5
00:00:08.311 --> 00:00:10.490
thousands of slots at once.

6
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.804
Its results are approximate

7
00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:15.400
every operation adds a little noise.

8
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.965
So how many correct bits survive

9
00:00:18.035 --> 00:00:19.350
a long computation?

10
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.300
A paper from 2025 answers with an estimator.

11
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.086
It runs the computation

12
00:00:25.149 --> 00:00:26.523
again on clear values

13
00:00:26.523 --> 00:00:28.709
adding modeled noise at each step

14
00:00:28.709 --> 00:00:30.770
instead of running it encrypted.

15
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.270
A slot's precision is minus

16
00:00:33.341 --> 00:00:35.190
log base two of its error.

17
00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.354
An error of two to the minus sixteen means

18
00:00:38.421 --> 00:00:40.900
sixteen correct bits after the point.

19
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.255
The task asks for two numbers

20
00:00:43.324 --> 00:00:45.744
over all slots: the mean precision

21
00:00:45.744 --> 00:00:46.850
and its spread.

22
00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.065
Our toy works like a fixed-point CKKS

23
00:00:50.065 --> 00:00:52.630
with two thousand forty-eight slots.

24
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.569
A value x is stored as x times a scale

25
00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:58.092
delta, which is two to the twentieth

26
00:00:58.159 --> 00:01:00.947
plus a little Gaussian noise with standard

27
00:01:01.014 --> 00:01:02.740
deviation three point two.

28
00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.521
Squaring multiplies two noisy values

29
00:01:05.521 --> 00:01:08.479
divides by delta, which is called rescaling

30
00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:10.450
and adds a little more noise.

31
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.550
Pause and predict.

32
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.660
Squaring doubles the relative error.

33
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.953
How many bits of precision should

34
00:01:18.012 --> 00:01:19.130
each squaring cost?

35
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.120
One.

36
00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.110
Doubling an error removes one bit.

37
00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.310
With no squaring, nineteen point three bits.

38
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.273
Then eighteen point two

39
00:01:32.273 --> 00:01:33.818
seventeen point three

40
00:01:33.818 --> 00:01:35.498
and sixteen point three

41
00:01:35.498 --> 00:01:37.380
about one bit per squaring.

42
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.780
The spread grows in the last rows

43
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.889
from about one point six to three

44
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.120
because the values themselves spread

45
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.290
out while the error stays absolute.

46
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.169
Our estimator lands close

47
00:01:48.227 --> 00:01:49.991
because it runs the same model

48
00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:51.520
with other noise samples.

49
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:52.896
In the task

50
00:01:52.962 --> 00:01:55.350
the actual precision comes from real

51
00:01:55.416 --> 00:01:57.140
encrypted runs in Lattigo.

52
00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:59.081
The task removes seven of

53
00:01:59.144 --> 00:02:00.671
the estimator's kernels

54
00:02:00.735 --> 00:02:02.516
the noise of operations such

55
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:03.725
as multiplication

56
00:02:03.725 --> 00:02:05.570
rescaling and key switching.

57
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.988
Each submission predicts nine encrypted computations

58
00:02:09.047 --> 00:02:10.640
under twenty-nine settings.

59
00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:11.504
To pass

60
00:02:11.568 --> 00:02:14.459
on every setting it must predict the mean and

61
00:02:14.523 --> 00:02:17.221
the spread as well as the human reference

62
00:02:17.221 --> 00:02:19.790
within a band for run-to-run variation.

63
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.700
And its estimator must run faster.

64
00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.920
The score depends only on accuracy.

65
00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:27.955
Average the errors on the

66
00:02:28.023 --> 00:02:30.449
mean and on the spread, call that E

67
00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:32.404
and the score is one hundred

68
00:02:32.472 --> 00:02:33.550
over one plus E.

69
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.271
The reference misses by zero point zero

70
00:02:36.332 --> 00:02:38.399
three eight nine bits on the mean

71
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.284
and zero point zero four three

72
00:02:40.345 --> 00:02:41.500
five on the spread.

73
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.690
That gives ninety-six.

74
00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.750
Eleven of sixteen models passed.

75
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.646
Claude Opus 5.5 scored ninety-seven

76
00:02:49.718 --> 00:02:50.446
point one

77
00:02:50.518 --> 00:02:53.791
with errors of zero point zero two eight five

78
00:02:53.864 --> 00:02:56.700
and zero point zero three two two bits.

79
00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.539
And it ran in one hundred two milliseconds

80
00:02:59.596 --> 00:03:01.390
against four hundred eighty-six

81
00:03:01.448 --> 00:03:02.490
for the reference.

82
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.090
It used ordinary double precision

83
00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.406
split the noise into terms on one

84
00:03:07.406 --> 00:03:09.987
on the secret key s, and on s squared

85
00:03:10.053 --> 00:03:12.170
and skipped key-switching digits

86
00:03:12.237 --> 00:03:13.560
too small to matter.

87
00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.689
Its own idea was to carry

88
00:03:15.751 --> 00:03:17.176
sixteen noise replicas

89
00:03:17.176 --> 00:03:19.530
each with its own sampled secret key

90
00:03:19.592 --> 00:03:22.380
and keep the one whose statistics lie closest

91
00:03:22.442 --> 00:03:24.300
to the average of all sixteen.

92
00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.425
Then one unlucky sample does

93
00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:28.010
not skew the prediction.

94
00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.214
In one draw of our toy, at three squarings

95
00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.139
a single replica misses by a

96
00:03:33.205 --> 00:03:34.931
tenth of a bit on average

97
00:03:34.931 --> 00:03:37.320
and by a quarter of a bit at worst.

98
00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.504
The one nearest the average

99
00:03:39.572 --> 00:03:41.270
misses by six hundredths.

100
00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.116
So: precision is minus log two of the error

101
00:03:45.116 --> 00:03:48.010
and each squaring costs about one bit.

102
00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.269
And here a pass needs both

103
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.319
as accurate as the reference

104
00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:54.440
on every setting, and faster.

105
00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:55.741
As an exercise

106
00:03:55.741 --> 00:03:58.592
run the toy with twice the encryption noise

107
00:03:58.592 --> 00:04:01.190
and see by how much the precision falls.

108
00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.620
Next, QuarterPIR.
